Draft: the skeleton of the article, on the template every article in
this group follows. `tools/articles.sh` skips it until the `draft` line
leaves the front matter. One article for both scale-mixture outcomes:
they share the sampler and the task and differ in the tail.

## Introduction

Errors as a scale mixture of normals (Andrews and Mallows 1974):
y_i | w_i ~ N(f(x_i), sigma^2 / w_i), with Gamma(df / 2, rate df / 2)
mixing for Student-t (Geweke 1993) and exponential mixing for Laplace
(the conditional of Park and Casella 2008). Each sweep redraws the
weights, then sigma^2, then the cells against precisions w_i / sigma^2:
a Gibbs scan with no acceptance-ratio term. Under Student-t a grid on df
gives an exact discrete conditional each sweep. Reach for them when a
few rows are far from the surface and the Gaussian model inflates sigma
to cover them.

Prerequisite: BART (Chipman, George and McCulloch 2010) and AddiVortes
(Stone and Gosling 2025).

## The model

The mixture, the two mixing distributions and the df grid, from
`docs/models.md`, "Student-t" and "Laplace". Link to
`crates/thiessen/src/models/student_t.rs`, `laplace.rs` and
`scale_mixture.rs`.

## Example

```{r example}
source("_setup.R")
data <- friedman(n = 300, p = 10, sd = 0.5, seed = 1, contaminate = 0.05)
control <- thiessen_control(tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule)
t_grid <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule,
  outcome = student_t_outcome(df = c(3, 6, 12))
)
laplace <- thiessen_control(
  tessellations = 50, general_params = schedule,
  outcome = laplace_outcome()
)
fits <- paired_fits(data, list(gaussian = control, student_t = t_grid, laplace = laplace), seeds)
summary(fits$student_t[[1]])
score(fits, data)
```

Five per cent of the training rows are moved by three noise standard
deviations; the held-out rows are clean. Comparator, one fit per seed:
Gaussian `BART::wbart` at matched size through `score(comparator = )`,
which arrives with this article, showing the default's failure is the
likelihood's and not the tessellation's. No BART-family CRAN package
ships a Student-t or Laplace error model; stochtree's custom-sampling
vignette builds one by hand and is the precedent.

## What it shows

Figure 1, mechanism: fitted against true on the held-out rows under the
three models, the contaminated training rows marked; second panel, the
posterior of `df` on the grid from `posterior::as_draws_df()` and of
`sigma` under all three.

```{r mechanism}
```

Figure 2, comparison: the three predictive intervals overlaid on a row
of held-out points, Gaussian widest, Laplace between, t narrowest,
beside the table (RMSE, coverage, width, log score, posterior mean of
sigma; the `wbart` row for RMSE, coverage and width).

```{r comparison}
```

Figure 3, boundary: the same three models at zero contamination; what
the heavier tails cost when nothing is an outlier, width and RMSE
relative to the Gaussian model.

```{r boundary}
```

## Validity

Stems `student_t` and `laplace`. SBC runs at fixed df; the grid
conditional is covered by the known-answer test. No broken-sampler
fixture, because no structural move gains an acceptance-ratio term.

```{r battery, results = "asis"}
cat(readLines("figures/heavy-tails/battery.md"), sep = "\n")
```

![SBC rank-ECDF difference, Student-t](figures/heavy-tails/sbc_ecdf_diff_student_t.png)

![Geweke, Student-t](figures/heavy-tails/geweke_ecdf_student_t.png)

![SBC rank-ECDF difference, Laplace](figures/heavy-tails/sbc_ecdf_diff_laplace.png)

![Geweke, Laplace](figures/heavy-tails/geweke_ecdf_laplace.png)

## Limits

State only what the module note supports. Candidates: sigma_hat for the
lambda prior is the least-squares residual standard deviation, which
overstates the scale under heavy tails, and the calibration rule has
not been restated for a scale mixture; df is fixed or on a grid because
a continuous-df sampler mixes poorly, and the grid prior is uniform
with no argument for its points; a variance ensemble is rejected
because per-observation weights and a per-observation variance product
both model dispersion and their joint identification awaits its
argument; the correspondence with the Gaussian model as df grows is in
distribution, not draw for draw.

## References

Andrews, D. F. and Mallows, C. L. (1974). Scale mixtures of normal
distributions. *Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B*
36(1), 99-102.

Chipman, H. A., George, E. I. and McCulloch, R. E. (2010). BART:
Bayesian additive regression trees. *The Annals of Applied Statistics*
4(1), 266-298. doi:10.1214/09-AOAS285

Geweke, J. (1993). Bayesian treatment of the independent Student-t
linear model. *Journal of Applied Econometrics* 8(S1), S19-S40.
doi:10.1002/jae.3950080504

Park, T. and Casella, G. (2008). The Bayesian lasso. *Journal of the
American Statistical Association* 103(482), 681-686.
doi:10.1198/016214508000000337

Stone, A. and Gosling, J. P. (2025). AddiVortes: (Bayesian) additive
Voronoi tessellations. *Journal of Computational and Graphical
Statistics* 34(3), 859-871. doi:10.1080/10618600.2024.2414104
